yoloctl local patch 0005: a raw-input attachment to the canonical main process.

An `openshell-main` attachment reads its client's input for a detach command
(openshell-supervisor-process src/ssh.rs, filter_main_detach_sequence): Ctrl-D
detaches and never reaches the main process, Ctrl-P is held until the next key
and Ctrl-P Ctrl-Q detaches. An agent running as the main process therefore
loses those keys.

A per-attachment switch, the env request OPENSHELL_MAIN_RAW_INPUT=1 (read where
OPENSHELL_MAIN_READ_ONLY already is), forwards every input byte unchanged and
never detaches on input. Such an attachment must own stdin: the subsystem
request succeeds only once it holds the stdin lease. When another attachment
owns stdin, the main process is unavailable, or OPENSHELL_MAIN_READ_ONLY=1 is
also requested, the request itself fails, followed by the existing "cannot
connect to the canonical main process" diagnostic and status 1. A client that
asked for raw input therefore never mistakes a refusal for an owned attachment
and is never a silent viewer. The lease's owner token is recorded on the
channel before any await, so dropping the connection's handler mid-request
releases it. Without the switch the behaviour is upstream's, unchanged.

